result:
*adjusts collar, speaks with unsettling calm*

You know what's interesting about payment systems? Everyone trusts them. They hand over their card, press a button, and believe the universe will sort it out fairly. That's... adorable.

Let me show you where the wolves hunt.

---

## The Test Suite They Don't Want You to Write

```go
// payment_processor_test.go

// === BASELINE TESTS (The Sheep Tests) ===

// 1. TestProcessPayment_Success - Happy path, money moves
// 2. TestProcessPayment_ZeroAmount - Amount <= 0, returns ErrInvalidAmount
// 3. TestProcessPayment_NegativeAmount - Negative amounts rejected
// 4. TestProcessPayment_ExceedsMaximum - Amount > 10000000 rejected
// 5. TestProcessPayment_CardExpired_PastYear - Old year fails
// 6. TestProcessPayment_CardExpired_SameYearPastMonth - Edge of expiry
// 7. TestProcessPayment_CardDeclined - Gateway says no
// 8. TestProcessPayment_GatewayError - Generic gateway failure
// 9. TestProcessPayment_RateLimited - Too many requests
// 10. TestProcessPayment_StoreSaveError - Database write fails
// 11. TestProcessPayment_StoreUpdateError_AfterSuccess - Charge worked, update didn't
// 12. TestRefundPayment_FullRefund - Complete refund flow
// 13. TestRefundPayment_PartialRefund - Take back some
// 14. TestRefundPayment_ExceedsOriginal - Can't refund more than paid
// 15. TestRefundPayment_PaymentNotFound - Ghost payment
// 16. TestRefundPayment_InvalidStatus_Pending - Can't refund pending
// 17. TestRefundPayment_InvalidStatus_Failed - Can't refund failed
// 18. TestRefundPayment_GatewayError - Refund gateway fails
// 19. TestGetPayment_Found - Retrieve existing payment
// 20. TestGetPayment_NotFound - Payment doesn't exist

// === WOLF TESTS (Where It Gets Interesting) ===

// 21. TestProcessPayment_IdempotencyKey_DuplicateRequest - Same key returns same result
// 22. TestProcessPayment_IdempotencyKey_Empty - Empty key processes normally
// 23. TestProcessPayment_IdempotencyKey_RaceCondition - Concurrent same-key requests
// 24. TestProcessPayment_CardExpired_CurrentMonthValid - This month should work
// 25. TestProcessPayment_CardNumber_LastFourDigitsOnly - Verify masking
// 26. TestProcessPayment_Metadata_ChargeIDStored - Verify charge_id in metadata
// 27. TestProcessPayment_CompletedAt_SetOnSuccess - Timestamp populated
// 28. TestProcessPayment_ContextCancellation - Context deadline exceeded
// 29. TestRefundPayment_MultiplePartials - Refund, refund, refund
// 30. TestRefundPayment_PartialThenFull - Partial then remainder
// 31. TestRefundPayment_StoreUpdateError - Refund worked, update failed
// 32. TestRefundPayment_StatusTransition_ToPartial - Completed → PartialRefund
// 33. TestRefundPayment_StatusTransition_ToFull - PartialRefund → Refunded
// 34. TestRefundPayment_ZeroAmount - Edge case: refund nothing?
// 35. TestProcessPayment_CurrencyHandling - Different currencies
// 36. TestProcessPayment_NilMetadata - No metadata provided
// 37. TestProcessPayment_CardNumberTooShort - Panics on slice?
// 38. TestProcessPayment_ConcurrentDifferentKeys - Parallel processing
// 39. TestRefundPayment_AlreadyFullyRefunded - Status is Refunded, try again
```

---

*leans forward*

That last one—test 37—that's the wolf hiding in the code. `req.CardNumber[len(req.CardNumber)-4:]` with a short string? Panic. Nobody validated that input.

See, most people write the first twenty. They test what the code *should* do. Wolves test what it *will* do when everything goes wrong at once.

There are two types of engineers, friend: those who test the edges, and those who get paged at 3 AM.

Which type are you?
